# Preferences

Living **user project preferences** — follow them by default. Prefer matching the user’s style; if a preference conflicts with a hard constraint (security, correctness, Next.js/App Router requirements), prioritize the constraint and note the trade-off.

Current preferences:

- One TSX file, one component: a `.tsx` UI module has exactly **one** function that returns JSX — the **default export** (name matches the file; see naming-convention-preferences). Do **not** add extra local/named React components in the same file (`FooView`, `FooInner`, `FooWithPath`, etc.), even if they are not exported. Allowed in that file: non-JSX helpers (plain functions/constants), hooks usage inside the single component, and co-located `types.ts`. Non-UI modules (`types.ts`, services, utils) are exempt. If a second UI component is truly needed, put it in its own folder/file under `components/`.
- Do not add re-export / barrel pass-through modules (`export … from "…"` that only forward another module). Import from the module that defines the symbol (e.g. `@/shared/services/types`, `@/back-end/common/errors`). Exception: UI kit barrels that re-export components defined in the same file (shadcn pattern) are fine.
- Treat `src/front-end/components/ui/*` (shadcn) as installed external third-party components. Do not modify, refactor, rename, or “clean up” them without the user’s explicit request or approval.
- Prefer array methods (`forEach`, `map`, `filter`, `reduce`, etc.) over `for...of` / classic `for` when iterating collections. Do not rewrite `forEach`/`map` into `for...of` for lint.
- Type-only imports must use `import type { ... }` (e.g. `import type { IData } from "@/shared/services/types"`).
- Component / feature props and related interfaces live in a co-located `types.ts` at the directory level (e.g. `src/front-end/components/budgets/types.ts`, `src/front-end/components/login/types.ts`, `src/app/login/types.ts`). Do not keep props interfaces inline in the component / page / layout file. Import with `import type { IBudgetsProps } from "@/front-end/components/budgets/types"`. Shared cross-cutting types stay in `@/shared/services/types` (or the module that owns them). App-route-only props (e.g. page `searchParams`) go in that route’s `types.ts` under `src/app/` — never inline in `page.tsx` / `layout.tsx`.
- Enforce layer split: `src/front-end/` (UI), `src/shared/` (isomorphic), `src/back-end/` (server). Allowed: FE → shared + server-actions; BE → shared + lib; shared → neither FE nor BE; app → FE + server-actions + controllers (RSC reads call controllers directly; mutations stay in server-actions). Forbidden: BE → FE, shared → FE/BE, FE → DAL/mappers/prisma/controllers.
- Server Actions (`"use server"` / `*SRF`) are for mutations and client-invoked calls only. RSC data reads go through controllers from `src/app` (not via get*SRF). Client table refetch (e.g. transactions pagination/filters) may use get*SRF; filters stay out of the URL by design — see `README-dev.md`.
- When project changes affect docs, update the matching README in the same change: [`README.md`](../../README.md) for public/onboarding (features, setup, scripts, pages, tech stack); [`README-dev.md`](../../README-dev.md) for architecture decisions, backend map, auth details, debug, experiments, roadmap. Do not dump dev-only notes into the main README.
- Logging: prefix every message with the logical area in brackets, e.g. `[auth] AUTH_EMAIL and AUTH_PASSWORD environment variables are not set`, `[pots] Failed to get pots data`.
- Class member order: all fields first, then all methods. Within fields and within methods: `public` → `protected` → `private`. Constructor sits with public methods (immediately after fields, before other public methods).
